---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import register from "../data/register.json";

const all = register.filter((r) => r.surname === "MORGILLO");
const byYear = {};
for (const r of all) {
  const y = (r.year || "").slice(0, 4);
  if (/^18\d\d$/.test(y)) byYear[y] = (byYear[y] || 0) + 1;
}
const years = Object.keys(byYear).sort();

// The Monticello de' Cappuccini house
const monticello = [
  ["Angelo Morgillo", "b. c. 1758", "colono", "strada Cappuccini", "Alive and declaring, 31 October 1828, aged seventy. Almost certainly the father of Nicola and Vincenzo, and of Saveria."],
  ["Nicola Morgillo", "b. c. 1794", "contadino", "Monticello de' Cappuccini", "34 in 1828. Married Maria Majone; their eight-year-old Raffaele died in May 1828. He declares his brother's child's death as «zio della defunta»."],
  ["Vincenzo Morgillo", "b. c. 1797", "contadino", "Monticello de' Cappuccini", "29 in May 1828, 32 in December. Married Felicetta Rivetti; their one-year-old Grazia died on 30 October 1828."],
  ["Saveria Morgillo", "b. c. 1779, d. 29 Nov 1827", "contadina", "strada Rejola", "«figlia di ANGELO MORGILLO». Married Carlantonio Porrino; buried a fourteen-month-old Raffaele in September 1826."],
];

// The strada Nuova di San Gaetano house
const gaetano = [
  ["Angelo Morgillo", "b. c. 1768, d. 4 Oct 1826", "contadino", "strada Nuova di San Gaetano", "«figlio di AMORE MORGILLO e di CATERINA LAUDATO». Married Marianna di Silvio. His son Biagio declared his death."],
  ["Biagio Morgillo", "b. c. 1796", "contadino", "strada Nuova di San Gaetano", "30 in 1826, when he declares his father's death as «figlio del defunto». Married Vincenza Vece; their 26-month-old Clementina died in March 1828."],
  ["Antonio Morgillo", "b. c. 1801", "contadino", "strada San Gaetano", "27 in March 1828, declaring his niece's death as «parente»."],
  ["Domenico Morgillo", "b. c. 1803", "contadino", "strada San Gaetano", "25 in September 1828, declaring as «parente»."],
  ["Simone Morgillo", "b. c. 1804", "contadino", "strada San Gaetano", "24 in March 1828, declaring as «parente»."],
];

// The generation whose parents the registers name
const elders = [
  ["Grazia Morgillo", "c. 1741 – 20 May 1826", "fu Nicola Morgillo & fu Orsola Diglio", "m. Giacinto Migliore"],
  ["Anna Morgillo", "c. 1743 – 3 Sep 1828", "fu Nicola Morgillo & fu Rosolina Bruno", "ved. Sabato Cossi"],
  ["Ovidia Morgillo", "c. 1736 – 14 Jun 1826", "fu Gennaro Morgillo & fu Maddalena d'Ambrosio", "ved. Raffaele — or Pasquale — Diglio"],
  ["Vittoria Morgillo", "c. 1798 – 4 Sep 1828", "fu Giuseppe Morgillo & Gelsomina Rivetti", "—"],
];
---
<Base title="Morgillo"
      eyebrow="The house the line married into"
      dek={`${all.length} Morgillo are recorded in this archive. Until 2026 almost none of them were joined to each other. The Arienzo death registers of 1826, 1827 and 1828, read act by act, put two Morgillo households on the ground for the first time.`}>

  <h2>Why the Morgillo matter</h2>
  <p><strong>Raffaele Falco, generation four, married Angela Rosa Morgillo</strong> — born at San Felice
  on 24 February 1818, daughter of Orazio, dead at Arienzo six days before her husband in April 1880.
  That marriage is the second of the two welds that make this family: the Crisci married in twice in
  generation two, the Morgillo once in generation four.</p>
  <p>But Angela Rosa arrived from outside. <strong>Orazio Morgillo's own house has never been
  reconstructed</strong>, and until the 1826–28 registers were read, the Morgillo of Arienzo were a
  scatter of names with no households behind them. They are now two houses, a hamlet and a street.</p>

  <h2>The house on Monticello de' Cappuccini</h2>
  <p>A hamlet on the hill above the town, beside the Capuchin convent. Its Morgillo and its Rivetti
  declare each other's deaths again and again.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Dates</th><th>Trade</th><th>Where</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{monticello.map(([n,d,t,w,e]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="small mono">{d}</td><td class="small">{t}</td>
        <td class="small">{w}</td><td class="small">{e}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Nicola and Vincenzo are proved brothers</strong> by a single word: on 31 October 1828
  Nicola declares the death of Vincenzo's one-year-old daughter as <em>«zio della defunta»</em> — her
  uncle. Angelo, seventy and a <em>colono</em> of the same hamlet, declares beside him.</p>

  <h2>The house on strada Nuova di San Gaetano</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Dates</th><th>Trade</th><th>Where</th><th>What the register says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{gaetano.map(([n,d,t,w,e]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="small mono">{d}</td><td class="small">{t}</td>
        <td class="small">{w}</td><td class="small">{e}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Antonio, Domenico and Simone all declare for Biagio's household as <em>parenti</em>, all live on
  the same street and all are within eight years of him. <strong>They are very probably his
  brothers</strong> — but no act says so, and this archive does not say so either.</p>

  <h2>Two men called Angelo Morgillo</h2>
  <p>This mattered more than it looks. In November 1827 <strong>Saveria Morgillo, forty-eight</strong>,
  died <em>«figlia di ANGELO MORGILLO»</em>. The only Angelo Morgillo then in the archive was the man
  buried in October 1826, given as <strong>fifty-eight</strong> — which would have made him
  <strong>eleven</strong> at her birth. The archive recorded the impossibility rather than explain it
  away.</p>
  <p>The 1828 register resolves it. On 31 October a <strong>second Angelo Morgillo, seventy, a
  <em>colono</em> of strada Cappuccini</strong>, walks into the town hall alive. Born about 1758, he is
  twenty-one at Saveria's birth. <strong>There are two men of that name</strong>, one on the hill and
  one on San Gaetano, and the age conflict was the evidence for it.</p>

  <h2>The generation the registers name backwards</h2>
  <p>Four Morgillo women died old enough that their acts name parents born in the seventeen-twenties and
  thirties — the deepest the civil series reaches for this surname.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Dates</th><th>Parents</th><th>Married</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{elders.map(([n,d,p,m]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="small mono">{d}</td><td class="small">{p}</td>
        <td class="small">{m}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Two Nicola Morgillo and a Gennaro</strong> stand at the head of these, all dead before 1826.
  <em>Amore Morgillo</em>, father of the Angelo of San Gaetano, is the strangest forename in the whole
  archive and ought to be findable in the parish books.</p>

  <h2>Two d'Addio women married two Morgillo men</h2>
  <p><strong>Rosa d'Addio</strong>, seventy, widow of <strong>Biagio Morgillo</strong>, died in the
  Ospedale di Ave Gratia Plena on 23 May 1828. <strong>Mariantonia d'Addio</strong>, thirty-one, a
  <em>filatrice</em> of strada Capodichina and wife of <strong>Pellegrino Morgillo</strong>, was
  murdered in the spring of 1827 — her body found buried in a wood, her face disfigured, part of the
  skull gone. The Royal Magistrate's own letter about it is sewn into the 1827 death register, asking
  the sindaco for a copy of the act <em>«per allegarla ad un processo criminale»</em>.
  <a href={u("/documents")}>The letter is on the Documents page.</a></p>

  <h2>Carlantonio Morgillo, three times over</h2>
  <p>There are <strong>at least three men called Carlantonio Morgillo</strong> alive in Arienzo in the
  eighteen-twenties: one of strada Capodiuomo who married Anna Arrigale, one of strada Capodichina aged
  thirty in 1828, and one of <strong>strada Camellara aged forty-six in 1826</strong>. The last of these
  is the one that matters — on <strong>28 October 1826</strong> he signed a death act
  <strong>beside Giuseppe Falco</strong>, both forty-six, both of Camellara. It is the first act in the
  whole Arienzo series in which a Falco and a Morgillo stand together, twenty-odd years before the two
  houses married.</p>

  <p><strong>The 1842 register names two of them in one line.</strong> On 1 June 1842
  <strong>Carlantonio Morgillo, seventy</strong>, a <em>contadino</em> of strada Porta di Sopra and
  widower of Rosa Fabio, died in the Ospedale d'Ave Gratia Plena — <em>«figlio di CARLANTONIO
  MORGILLO defunto, di professione contadino»</em>. <strong>Father and son of one name, in one
  household.</strong> The seventy-year-old was born about 1772, which makes him too old to be the
  man of thirty in 1828 and a plausible match for the forty-six-year-old of 1826 — <em>plausible,
  and nothing more</em>. Which of the three men above these two are is <strong>not settled
  here</strong>, and the archive is not going to settle it by arithmetic.</p>

  <h2>Eleven more Morgillo, from 1841, 1842 and 1843</h2>
  <p>The three volumes read in 2026 name Morgillo on almost every street in Arienzo. They are set
  out here not because they connect to this family — <strong>none of them does</strong> — but for
  the opposite reason. The surname was <em>common</em>. A Morgillo in a record proves nothing on its
  own, and this table is the evidence for that caution.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Act</th><th>Who</th><th>What the act says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono">1843, atti 49 &amp; 54</td><td><strong>Francesca</strong>, 56, and <strong>Nicola</strong>, 50</td><td><strong>Sister and brother</strong>, dead nine days apart, both <em>«figli di GAETANO MORGILLO defunto e MARIA CIOFFI defunta»</em>, strada Capodiconca. The repetition is what settles the mother's surname.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1842, atto 7</td><td><strong>Angela</strong>, 80</td><td><em>«figlia di SIMONE MORGILLO defunto e VITTORIA DIGLIO defunta»</em>, widow of Gaspare Ummarino. Born about 1762. A <strong>Simone Morgillo</strong> is one of the San Gaetano brothers above — same name, right generation, <strong>not merged</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1842, atto 31</td><td><strong>Carlantonio</strong>, 70</td><td>Son of <strong>Carlantonio Morgillo</strong>; died in the ospedale. See above.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1841 atto 21; 1841 atto 82</td><td><strong>Nicola</strong>, 55 and 50</td><td>A <em>colono</em> of Capodiconca and a <em>calzolajo</em> of <strong>strada CAMELLARA</strong>. Different streets, different trades, five years apart in age. <strong>Two men, or one clerk guessing twice.</strong> Not merged — and the second is a Morgillo living on the Falco street.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1841 atto 19; 1841 atto 54; 1843 atto 38</td><td><strong>Gennaro</strong>, <em>vaticale</em></td><td>Of strada Sant'Andrea, across three acts. His son <strong>Simone</strong> died at sixteen months in 1841. The register makes him <strong>45 in 1841 and 40 in 1843</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1841 atto 71; 1842 atto 80</td><td><strong>Maria Giuseppa</strong></td><td>Wife of Angelo Cioffi of strada Cioffi. The 1842 act writes her simply «Maria Morgillo»; the 1841 act gives the full name. <strong>She is not the Maria Morgillo who married Luca Zimbardo</strong> — that is a different woman, on a different street.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1841 atto 47; 1842 atto 46</td><td><strong>Maria</strong></td><td>Wife of <strong>Luca Zimbardo</strong>, strada Capodiconca. They buried <strong>two sons, both named Antonio</strong> — one at sixteen months in August 1841, one at twenty-two days in August 1842. The name was given again after the first died.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1841, atto 66</td><td><strong>Giovanna</strong>, 10</td><td>Daughter of <strong>Pasquale Morgillo</strong> and Carmina Cioffi, strada Cioffi.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1842, atto 81</td><td><strong>Costantina</strong>, 16; <strong>Marco</strong></td><td>Daughter of <strong>Leopoldo Morgillo</strong> and Giulia Petrella, strada Capodiconca; Marco Morgillo declares.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1842, atto 85; 1842 atto 11</td><td><strong>Giovanni</strong>, 55</td><td>With <strong>Lucia di Lucia</strong>; their son Antonio died at eleven. He declares on strada Cioffi in February and is named as a father on strada Santa Lucia in December.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono">1842 atti 12, 24, 52, 60; 1841 atti 9, 23, 50</td><td><strong>Teresa · Giuseppe · Giuditta · Maddalena · Andrea · Angela</strong></td><td>Scattered singletons — a mother on Porta di Sopra, a declarant on strada Costa, a dead mother, a wife on Capodiconca, a declarant of 32 on strada Cioffi, a wife on Terra Murata. Recorded, unjoined.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small"><strong>Angela Rosa Morgillo, who married Raffaele Falco, is not among them</strong>,
  and no act in these three volumes reaches her. Her father Orazio was of San Felice a Cancello, whose
  registers this archive has still not opened.</p>

  <h2>Every Morgillo this archive holds</h2>
  <p class="small">{all.length} entries. Most are not kin to this family and are here because they were
  found. <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Morgillo"}>Open them in the register →</a></p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Entries</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{years.map((y) => (<tr><td class="mono">{y}</td><td class="mono">{byYear[y]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What would settle the rest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Orazio Morgillo of San Felice.</strong> Angela Rosa's father. San Felice a Cancello is a
    separate comune with its own registers, none of which this archive has read.</li>
    <li><strong>The Arienzo marriage registers.</strong> A marriage act names both fathers. One would
    join the Monticello brothers to Angelo, and the San Gaetano brothers to Biagio, in a line of print.</li>
    <li><strong>Amore Morgillo.</strong> A forename that rare should be traceable in the parish books
    back into the seventeen-hundreds.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
